  "text_md": "# Creati ng we ll defined st reet s and spa ce s\n\n### Are buildings designed and positioned with landscaping to define and enhance\n\n### streets and spaces and are buildings designed to turn street corners well?\n\n**7a** Good streets and spaces are created by enclosing **7b** Good buildings ‘turn’ corners. them with buildings and a strong landscaping Do buildings turn corners well? scheme. Are buildings used to create enclosed streets and spaces? **7c** Do all fronts of buildings, including front doors, face the street?\n\n## We recommend\n\nOrientating front doors to face the street rather than being tucked around the back or sides of buildings. Creating streets that are principally defined by the position of buildings rather than the route of the carriageway. **We recommend that you avoid**\n\n●\n\n|Designing building that turn corners well, so that both||||Streets that lack spatial enclosure by exceeding|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|elevations seen from the street have windows to them,||||recommended height to width ratios.|\n|rather than offering blank walls to the street|||. Consider||\n|using windows that wrap around corners to maximise||||Over reliance on in front of plot parking that tends to|\n|surveillance and bring generous amounts of natural light||||create over wide streets dominated by parked cars and|\n|into people’s homes.||||driveways unless there is sufficient space to use strong and extensive landscaping to compensate the lack of built|\n|Using a pattern of road types streets and consider their enclosure, keeping to the well||to create a hierarchy of||form enclosure.|\n|proportioned height to width ratios relative to the type of||||Homes that back on to the street, or offer a blank|\n|street|.|Minimum|Maximum|elevation to the street. Using garages and/or driveways or service areas to turn|\n|Minor streets, e.g. mews||1: 1.15|1:1|corners.|\n|Typical streets||1: 3|1: 1.5||\n|Squares||1: 6|1: 4||\n\n14 ●\n\n● 15\n\n●\n\n*Source: Manual